Create a file named `insert_data.php`. This script will handle the incoming HTTP POST request, insert the received data into the database, and then redirect back to the original page after successful insertion.

Here's an example of what `insert_data.php` might look like:

```php
<?php
// Database connection parameters
$servername = "localhost";
$username = "your_username"; // Replace with your actual username
$password = "your_password"; // Replace with your actual password
$dbname = "Bob";

// Create connection
$conn = new mysqli($servername, $username, $password, $dbname);

// Check connection
if ($conn->connect_error) {
    die("Connection failed: " . $conn->connect_error);
}

// Get POST data
$field1 = isset($_POST['field1']) ? $_POST['field1'] : '';
$field2 = isset($_POST['field2']) ? $_POST['field2'] : '';

// Sanitize input data
$field1 = htmlspecialchars($field1);
$field2 = htmlspecialchars($field2);

// Prepare and bind
$stmt = $conn->prepare("INSERT INTO records (data) VALUES (?)");
$stmt->bind_param("s", $data);

// Set parameters and execute
$data = $field1 . ' - ' . $field2;
if ($stmt->execute()) {
    echo "<p>Data inserted successfully!</p>";
} else {
    echo "<p>Error inserting data: " . $stmt->error . "</p>";
}

// Close statement and connection
$stmt->close();
$conn->close();

// Redirect back to the form page
header('Location: index.html'); // Change 'index.html' to your form page name
exit();
?>
```

### 4. Create the Form Page (`index.html`)

