To manage the maximum number of concurrent coroutines in PHP 8.2, you can use several approaches depending on your specific requirements and environment setup. Here are some strategies:

1. **Use `set_time_limit()`**: This function sets the maximum execution time for a script. If a script exceeds this limit, it will terminate all active processes, including coroutines.

   ```php
   set_time_limit(30); // Set timeout to 30 seconds
   ```

2. **Limit Coroutine Creation**: You can create a mechanism to ensure that no more than five coroutines are running at any given time. One way to do this is by using a counter or a queue system.

   ```php
   $activeCoroutines = [];

   function startCoroutine($coroutine) {
       if (count($activeCoroutines) >= 5) {
           throw new Exception("Maximum number of coroutines reached.");
       }
       $activeCoroutines[] = $coroutine;
       $coroutine->start();
   }

   class Coroutine {
       public function __construct(callable $callback) {
           $this->callback = $callback;
       }

       public function run() {
           call_user_func($this->callback);
       }
   }

   try {
       $coroutine1 = new Coroutine(function () {
           echo "Coroutine 1\n";
           sleep(5);
       });
       startCoroutine($coroutine1);

       $coroutine2 = new Coroutine(function () {
           echo "Coroutine 2\n";
           sleep(5);
       });
       startCoroutine($coroutine2);

       // Add more coroutines as needed...
   } catch (Exception $e) {
       echo $e->getMessage();
   }
   ```

3. **Use a Queue System**: Implement a queue system where each coroutine enqueues tasks instead of starting them directly. A worker process or thread then picks up these tasks one by one until the queue is empty.

4. **Monitor and Terminate Coroutines**: Regularly monitor the status of coroutines and terminate those that exceed the allowed count.

By combining these methods, you can effectively control the concurrency level in your application while ensuring stability and performance.

To prevent XPath injection vulnerabilities, you should use parameterized queries or prepared statements instead of directly concatenating user input into your XPath expressions. Here's an example using Java with JAXP (Java API for XML Processing):

```java
import javax.xml.xpath.XPath;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathConstants;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathExpressionException;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathFactory;

public class XpathExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws XPathExpressionException {
        // Create an instance of XPathFactory
        XPathFactory factory = XPathFactory.newInstance();

        // Get an XPath object from the factory
        XPath xpath = factory.newXPath();

        // Define the safe XPath expression
        String expression = "/users/user[@name=? and @pass=?]";

        // Prepare the parameters safely
        Object[] params = {user, pass};

        // Evaluate the expression with the parameters
