            @Override
            public Source getSystemId() {
                return null; // No need to provide a system ID here
            }

            @Override
            public Source getContent() {
                return null; // No content needed
            }
        }, XPathConstants.STRING);

        System.out.println("Result: " + result);
    }
}
```

In this example, we create a safe XPath expression by replacing the literal values (`"/users/user[@name='...' and @pass='...']"`) with placeholders (`?`). We then prepare the parameters (`user` and `pass`) separately and evaluate the expression using these parameters.

This approach ensures that user input is properly sanitized and prevents potential XPath injection attacks.

